hi this is presh talwalkar i'm going to  teach you how you can multiply numbers  by drawing lines let's say you want to  multiply 12 by 13.  for the one we'll draw one line and then  for the two we'll leave some space and  draw two lines for the other number  we'll draw lines in the opposite  direction  now we will group intersections that are  vertically aligned and count the number  of intersections in each group  there are six intersections on the right  five in the center and 1 on the left  we can combine these numbers to get to  our answer of 156  we can use this technique even for  larger numbers  for 123 times 321  we use exactly the same process of  drawing lines for each digit of the  number  for the other number we will draw lines  in the opposite direction making sure to  leave space between the lines for each  digit  now we will group intersections that are  vertically aligned and then count the  number of intersections in each group  the center group has a lot of  intersections we want to count them  carefully they're fourteen  before we get to the answer we have to  make one adjustment we need to carry the  one of the fourteen over to the eight  and eight plus one makes nine  now we have our answer of thirty nine  thousand four hundred and eighty three  you can also multiply numbers by drawing  lines for numbers that include a digit  zero  we'll simply draw  the line for zero in a different color  if you don't have a different color  you can use a dashed line just to know  that the zero line is different  after we group vertically aligned  intersections we count the number of  intersections in each group  but we make one adjustment we don't  count any of the intersections with the  zero line because zero times anything is  zero  and that gets us to our answer of twenty  two thousand seven hundred and sixty  three we can also do polynomial  expressions x plus two times x plus  three  we will draw the lines for x in a  different color  now we group the vertically aligned  intersections on the far right there are  six between  unit lines in the middle there are five  between a red and a black line which  makes it 5x  an intersection of two red lines is an x  squared  which gets us our answer of x squared  plus five x plus six  please subscribe to my channel i make  videos on math and game theory you can  support me on patreon catch me on my  blog mind your decisions on twitter at  preshtalwalker and you can get please  get my book on multiplying numbers by  drawing lines
 

